+
+## 2. What is genuinely strong
+
+Worth saying plainly, because the execution order below is otherwise all bad news.
+
+- **The honesty discipline is already in place.** `lib/simulation.ts:17-27` states in the
+  file itself that no FloorForge machine has ever refinished a floor and that every number
+  is a design target plus bounded noise. `app/robots.ts:39` keeps the mock dashboard out of
+  the index. That instinct is the hardest thing to retrofit and it is already here.
+- **The unit reconciliation was done right.** `lib/types.ts:220-236` — the `avg_pressure_bar`
+  → `avg_pressure_psi` fix, with the 14.5038× failure mode written down. That is the
+  standard the rest of the backend has to meet.
+- **One job record already exists** (`lib/jobs.ts:14-30`) and deliberately mirrors
+  `types.Job`. The canonical-object work is half-done and pointed the right way.
+- **`lib/pathPlanning.ts` is real coverage planning**, not a decorative animation.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The breaks
+
+### 3.1 · P0 — Telemetry ingest silently destroys the 1 Hz pressure stream
+
+The single highest-value stream in the product cannot be stored.
+
+| Layer | Accepts `pressure_reading`? |
+|---|---|
+| `lib/types.ts:191-209` — `EventType` union | **yes** (15 types) |
+| `lib/validators.ts:447-462` — `isValidEventType` | **yes** (15 types) |
+| `migrations/001_initial_schema.sql:83-95` — PG `event_type` enum | **no** (11 types) |
+
+Reproduce:
+
+```bash
+node -e '
+const fs=require("fs");
+const ts=[...fs.readFileSync("lib/types.ts","utf8")
+  .match(/export type EventType =([\s\S]*?);/)[1].matchAll(/"([a-z_]+)"/g)].map(m=>m[1]);
+const sql=[...fs.readFileSync("migrations/001_initial_schema.sql","utf8")
+  .match(/CREATE TYPE event_type AS ENUM \(([\s\S]*?)\);/)[1].matchAll(/'"'"'([a-z_]+)'"'"'/g)].map(m=>m[1]);
+console.log("accepted by API, rejected by database:", ts.filter(t=>!sql.includes(t)));
+'
+# accepted by API, rejected by database:
+#   [ 'pressure_reading', 'coverage_checkpoint', 'job_paused', 'job_resumed' ]
+```
+
+The four missing values are exactly the four the firmware contract owns
+(`SOFTWARE_HARDWARE_CONTRACT.md:103`). The July fix added them to TypeScript and to the
+validator and stopped there. So the failure mode moved rather than closing: a device
+following the contract used to be rejected at validation with a clean `400 Invalid event
+type`; it is now **accepted at validation and rejected by Postgres**, which surfaces at
+`app/api/telemetry/route.ts:70-84` as a `500 INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR`. There is no retry
+contract, no buffer, no dead-letter. The event is gone.
+
+**What is actually lost.** `lib/simulation.ts:322-327` — the reference telemetry producer,
+the one written so that "the day a real D1 posts to /api/telemetry, nothing downstream
+changes" (`:35`) — emits five event types:
+
+| Emitted at | Type | Storable |
+|---|---|---|
+| `simulation.ts:303` | `pass_started` | yes |
+| `simulation.ts:322` | `pressure_reading` — **1 Hz** | **no** |
+| `simulation.ts:323` | `dust_reading` — 1 Hz | yes |
+| `simulation.ts:327` | `coverage_checkpoint` — ~5 min | **no** |
+| `simulation.ts:343` | `pass_completed` | yes |
+
+Two of five, including **100 % of the pressure samples**. Pressure against species,
+grit, speed and surface response *is* the dataset this company calls its moat. At the
+first hop, the moat is a `500`.
+
+The sentence at `simulation.ts:35` is not true today. Making it true is Mission 1.
+
+---
+
+### 3.2 · P0 — Row Level Security denies every write in the product
+
+`migrations/001_initial_schema.sql:274-280` enables RLS on all seven tables.
+`:283-347` then defines seven policies. All seven are `FOR SELECT`.
+
+```bash
+grep -c "FOR SELECT" migrations/001_initial_schema.sql   # 7
+grep -c "FOR INSERT" migrations/001_initial_schema.sql   # 0
+grep -c "FOR UPDATE" migrations/001_initial_schema.sql   # 0
+grep -c "FOR ALL"    migrations/001_initial_schema.sql   # 0
+```
+
+RLS enabled with no permissive policy for a verb denies that verb. There is no
+`INSERT`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE` policy on any table, and the application connects with the
+**anon** key (`lib/db/client.ts:10-22`). Against a correctly migrated database:
+
+- `POST /api/applications` — every pilot lead — fails.
+- `POST /api/jobs` — every job — fails.
+- `POST /api/telemetry` — every event — fails.
+
+The read policies do not work either: they resolve `auth.uid()`, which is `NULL` for an
+anon key, so each `USING` clause evaluates to `NULL` and denies.
+
+This is invisible today only because Supabase is not configured and
+`lib/db/client.ts:19-20` silently substitutes `https://example.supabase.co`. **The first
+day real credentials are added, the entire write side of the product stops**, and the
+failure will look like a hundred unrelated 500s rather than one missing migration.
+
+The architectural fix is not "add anon write policies". Devices must never hold a
+database-writing key. The server authenticates the caller and writes with a service-role
+client that is never bundled to the browser. See §4, Mission 1.
+
+---
+
+### 3.3 · P0 — CI is red on `main`, so no gate has run in weeks
+
+```bash
+node scripts/check-repo-hygiene.mjs; echo "exit=$?"
+# ✗ 1 patch file(s) tracked in the repository:
+#     FLOORFORGE_46_database_not_configured.patch
+# exit=1
+```
+
+`.github/workflows/verify.yml:53-54` runs hygiene as step 2 of 8. It exits 1, so
+**`typecheck`, `lint`, `build` and the audit scripts have not run on `main`.** The
+workflow's own header comment (`:7-9`) says this gate exists because "a delivery patch
+file reached `main` twice through the GitHub web UI". It reached `main` a third time and
+the gate that was built to catch it is now the reason nothing else is being checked.
+
+For the record, both gates behind it currently pass — `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean and
+`npx eslint` reports one warning, zero errors. The damage is not a hidden type error; it
+is that **nobody can know that**, and that a genuinely red build has been normalised.
+
+---
+
+### 3.4 · P0 — No authentication or authorisation exists on any API route
+
+```bash
+grep -rn -i "service_role\|api_key\|Authorization\|bearer" --include=*.ts app lib
+# (no matches)
+```
+
+Not "weak". Absent. Concretely:
+
+- **`GET /api/applications` (`app/api/applications/route.ts:62-68`)** returns the entire
+  pilot pipeline — name, email, phone, company, monthly sqft target, internal notes — to
+  anyone who requests it. The route's own comment reads *"TODO: Add auth check… For now,
+  this is open for demo purposes."* This is the company's sales pipeline and it is
+  `curl`-able. `proxy.ts:10-14` protects `/dashboard(.*)` only; the matcher at `:23` includes
+  `/api/*` but `auth.protect()` is never called for it.
+- **`GET /api/jobs` (`:81-95`)** takes `tenant_id` from a **query parameter**. The caller
+  chooses whose data to read.
+- **`GET /api/jobs/[id]` (`app/api/jobs/[id]/route.ts:22-24`)** applies no tenant filter
+  at all — `db.getJobById(id)` is called with one argument although the helper accepts a
+  `tenant_id` (`lib/db/client.ts:149-165`).
+- **`POST /api/telemetry`** accepts events for any `job_id` and any `robot_id` from any
+  caller on the internet. Quality evidence, warranty evidence and the training corpus are
+  all forgeable by design.
+
+The last one is the one that matters commercially. A digital job passport is worth
+exactly as much as the difficulty of forging it, and today that is zero.
+
+---
+
+### 3.5 · P1 — `PATCH /api/jobs/[id]` bypasses its own validator
+
+`lib/validators.ts:248-320` defines `validateJobUpdate`, which checks status against the
+enum and bounds `coverage_pct` and `approval_score`. `app/api/jobs/[id]/route.ts:56-76`
+does not call it. It hand-rolls an allowlist and writes `allowedUpdates.status =
+body.status` **with no enum check**, so `{"status":"definitely_finished"}` reaches
+Postgres. It also skips `current_pass`, which the validator handles.
+
+The state machine is in the same condition. `migrations/001:380-406` writes out nine
+transition rules as a comment and then enforces exactly one of them (no transition out of
+`archived`). A job can go `draft → approved` without ever having run.
+
+---
+
+### 3.6 · P1 — No provenance column anywhere
+
+`lib/simulation.ts` emits events in the exact shape of the firmware contract, into the
+same endpoint, with the same event types. Nothing in `telemetry_events`
+(`migrations/001:237-247`) distinguishes them.
+
+The moment both a simulator and a machine post to the same table, the dataset is
+permanently inadmissible: no query can separate what was measured from what was modelled,
+and every quality claim built on it becomes unprovable. This is cheap to fix now and
+impossible to fix retroactively.
+
+---
+
+### 3.7 · P0 — No tests
+
+No test runner, no test script, no test file.
+
+```bash
+grep -c '"test"' package.json   # 0
+find . -name '*.test.*' -not -path './node_modules/*' | wc -l   # 0
+```
+
+§3.1 is a two-line contract drift between two files in the same repository. It survived a
+dedicated product-truth audit that cited both files, because no instrument compares them.
+Every finding above is of that class.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Execution order
+
+**P0 — blocks product, pilot, safety, data integrity or revenue**
+
+1. **The ingest path.** §3.1 + §3.2 + §3.4 + §3.6 are one mission, because none of them
+   can be fixed alone: the enum fix does nothing while RLS denies the insert, RLS cannot
+   be opened without a device identity to open it for, and a device identity is what
+   makes provenance non-forgeable. Ship it with the dedup and resume semantics an
+   offline-first machine needs, or it will be rebuilt in six months.
+2. **CI green** (§3.3) — one file. Do it first; nothing else is verifiable without it.
+3. **Test infrastructure** (§3.7), starting with a contract test that fails when
+   TypeScript and SQL disagree about the event vocabulary.
+4. **Tenant isolation and the job state machine** (§3.4, §3.5).
+
+**P1 — materially improves autonomy, quality, economics or adoption**
+
+5. FloorTwin as the canonical floor object; `lib/floorPlan.ts` is the seed.
+6. Process planner: species + condition + target quality → executable plan.
+7. Reconcile the four throughput numbers (`audit/PRODUCT_TRUTH.md` T0-2) — the planner
+   cannot be honest while the product has four answers to "how long does my floor take".
+8. ROI calculator claim ceiling (`PRODUCT_TRUTH.md` T0-1).
+
+**P2 — strategically useful later**
+
+Fleet OS, maintenance intelligence, closed-loop sanding, MCP tool surface, `/api/v1`,
+inspection, replay and fault injection in the simulator.
+
+**P3 — distraction**
+
+Billing, SSO, enterprise administration, third-party hardware, marketplace. Nothing here
+is touched until a machine has refinished a floor for a paying contractor.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Mission 1, stated precisely
+
+> Make `POST /api/telemetry` an interface a real machine can be built against: authenticated
+> per device, storable for every event type the firmware contract defines, idempotent under
+> at-least-once delivery, resumable after a network outage, provenance-stamped so simulated
+> and measured data can never be confused, and proven by tests that fail when any of those
+> properties regresses.
+
+Everything else in this document waits.
